From d535692773e4ba0b9080defec0719c12f78ff830 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:29:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/32] gpu: add gpu_wait_reg_mem_high_res for sub-millisecond
 WAIT_REG_MEM polling

While a WAIT_REG_MEM condition is unmet and vsync is on, the command
processor sleeps wait / 0x100 ms between polls, which with a 1 ms timer
period is 1 to 2 ms, and the guest's progress (often a vblank or a
fence it waits on) is seen that much later.

With gpu_wait_reg_mem_high_res (default off) it polls every 0.5 ms
instead, on a new PreciseSleep helper private to the GPU module. On
Windows that is a CREATE_WAITABLE_TIMER_HIGH_RESOLUTION timer. Under
Wine a waitable timer is a wineserver object, so each sleep was a
set_timer request plus a server wakeup, 987 a second in a measured
Proton run on the Steam Deck; Wine's NtDelayExecution sleeps in the
process, so under Wine (detected by ntdll's wine_get_version) that is
used instead. A wineserver trace afterwards showed no requests from the
GPU command thread. Elsewhere it is std::this_thread::sleep_for. When no
precise sleep is available the old sleep is kept.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
